WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.233 --> 00:00:04.966
The greatest people
are self-managing.

00:00:04.966 --> 00:00:06.166
They don’t need
to be managed.

00:00:06.166 --> 00:00:07.599
If they know--

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.400
Once they know
what to do,

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.833
they’ll go figure out
how to do it,

00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:12.266
and they don’t need
to be managed at all.

00:00:12.266 --> 00:00:14.899
What they need
is a common vision,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.466
and that’s what
leadership is.

00:00:16.466 --> 00:00:18.666
What leadership
is having a vision,

00:00:18.666 --> 00:00:22.266
being able to articulate that so the
people around you can understand it,

00:00:22.266 --> 00:00:25.366
and getting a consensus
on a common vision.

00:00:25.366 --> 00:00:29.332
We wanted people that were
insanely great at what they did,

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.433
but were not necessarily
those seasoned professionals,

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.066
but who had at the
tips of their fingers

00:00:36.066 --> 00:00:40.132
and in their passion the latest
understanding of where technology was

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.999
and what we could do
with that technology,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.100
and we wanted to bring
that to lots of people.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.633
So the neatest thing
that happens

00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:50.033
is when you get a core group of,
you know, ten great people,

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:54.033
it becomes self-policing as
to who they let into that group.

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:59.433
So I consider the most important job
of someone like myself is recruiting.

00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.233
We agonized over hiring.

00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:02.566
We had interviews.

00:01:02.566 --> 00:01:04.832
I'd go back and look at some
of the interviews again.

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:08.066
They would start at 9:00 or 10:00 in
the morning and go through dinner.

00:01:08.066 --> 00:01:11.866
A new interviewee would talk to
everybody in the building at least once

00:01:11.866 --> 00:01:13.299
and maybe a couple times,

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.266
and then come back for
another round of interviews,

00:01:15.266 --> 00:01:17.032
and then we’d all get together
and talk about it.

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:18.833
And then they'd fill
out an application.

00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:19.933
[laughing]

00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:21.033
No, they never filled out an application.

00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:22.899
The critical part of the interview,
at least to my mind,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.066
was when we finally decided
we liked them enough

00:01:25.066 --> 00:01:27.232
to show them the
Macintosh prototype

00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:28.899
and then we sat them
down in front of it.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.766
If they were just kind of bored,
or said “This is a nice computer,”

00:01:31.766 --> 00:01:32.866
we didn’t want them.

00:01:32.866 --> 00:01:34.432
We wanted their
eyes to light up

00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:35.899
and for them to
get really excited,

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.566
and then we knew
they were one of us.

00:01:37.566 --> 00:01:39.099
And everybody
just wanted to work.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.233
Not because it was work
that had to be done,

00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:43.966
but it was because something
we really believed in

00:01:43.966 --> 00:01:45.866
that was just going to
really make a difference.

00:01:45.866 --> 00:01:47.366
And that’s what kept
the whole thing going.

00:01:47.366 --> 00:01:48.966
We all wanted exactly
the same thing,

00:01:48.966 --> 00:01:53.299
instead of spending our time arguing
about what the computer should be.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.966
We all knew what
the computer should be,

00:01:54.966 --> 00:01:56.366
and we just went
and did it.

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:58.466
We went through that stage in Apple
where we went out and thought oh,

00:01:58.466 --> 00:02:01.299
we’re gonna be a big company,
let’s hire professional management.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.233
We went out and hired a bunch
of professional management--

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.566
It didn’t work at all.

00:02:05.566 --> 00:02:06.899
Most of them were bozos.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.266
They knew how
to manage,

00:02:08.266 --> 00:02:10.032
but they didn’t know
how to do anything!

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:11.899
And so, if you’re
a great person,

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.666
why do you want to work for somebody
you can’t learn anything from?

00:02:14.666 --> 00:02:17.699
And you know
what’s interesting,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.666
you know who the
best managers are?

00:02:19.666 --> 00:02:22.199
They’re the great
individual contributors,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.066
who never ever
want to be a manager,

00:02:25.066 --> 00:02:27.066
but decide they
have to be a manager

00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:31.332
because no one else is going to
be able to do as good a job as them.

00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:33.299
[male narrator] After hiring two
professional managers

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.966
from outside the company
and firing them both,

00:02:35.966 --> 00:02:40.599
Jobs gambled on Debby Coleman,
a member of the Macintosh team.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:41.833
Thirty-two years old,

00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:44.866
an English Literature major
with an MBA from Stanford,

00:02:44.866 --> 00:02:49.832
Debbie was a financial manager
with no experience in manufacturing.

00:02:49.833 --> 00:02:53.266
I mean, there’s no way
in the world anybody else

00:02:53.266 --> 00:02:55.566
would give me this chance
to run this kind of operation,

00:02:55.566 --> 00:02:57.399
and I don’t kid myself
about that.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.000
It’s an incredible,
high risk

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.066
both for myself, personally
and professionally,

00:03:01.066 --> 00:03:02.366
and for Apple
as a company,

00:03:02.366 --> 00:03:04.566
to put a person like
myself in this job.

00:03:04.566 --> 00:03:07.499
I mean, they’re really
betting on a lot of things.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.200
We’re betting that my skills
at organizational effectiveness,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.266
you know, override
all lack of technology,

00:03:14.266 --> 00:03:18.599
lack of experience, lack of,
you know, time in manufacturing.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:19.666
So, it’s a big risk,

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:23.566
and I’m just an example in every
single person on the Mac team,

00:03:23.566 --> 00:03:25.932
almost to your
entry-level person,

00:03:25.933 --> 00:03:27.033
you could say
that about.

00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:32.299
This is a place where people were
afforded incredibly unique opportunities

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.833
to prove that they could do--

00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:36.133
--they could write
the book again.

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:39.133
[narrator] Inscribed inside the
casing of every Macintosh,

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:40.766
unseen by the consumer,

00:03:40.766 --> 00:03:43.099
are the signatures
of the whole team.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.700
This is Apple’s way of affirming
that their latest innovation

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.933
is a product of the individuals
who created it,

00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:49.699
not the corporation.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.600
	

